[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Designing End-to-End Recommendation Systems"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Recommendation systems are a fundamental component of modern data science applications, driving user engagement and personalization across e-commerce, streaming platforms, and social media. This tutorial covers the end-to-end design process, moving from data ingestion and offline training to online serving and evaluation."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. System Architecture Overview"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"An industrial-grade recommendation system typically follows a multi-stage pipeline architecture to handle millions of items efficiently in real-time. The core stages include candidate generation (retrieval), scoring (ranking), and re-ranking."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Candidate Generation: Narrows down the item pool from millions to hundreds using fast retrieval methods like collaborative filtering or Two-Tower neural networks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Scoring and Ranking: Evaluates the candidate subset using complex machine learning models incorporating rich feature sets to predict exact interaction probabilities."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Re-ranking: Applies business logic, diversity constraints, freshness filtering, and deduplication before presenting results to the user."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Candidate Generation with Two-Tower Models"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Two-Tower architectures separate user features and item features into distinct neural networks, generating dense embeddings that can be efficiently searched using Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) libraries like Faiss."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"import tensorflow as tf

class UserTower(tf.keras.Model):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.dense = tf.keras.layers.Dense(64, activation='relu')

    def call(self, inputs):
        return self.dense(inputs)

class ItemTower(tf.keras.Model):
    def __init__(self):
        super().__init__()
        self.dense = tf.keras.layers.Dense(64, activation='relu')

    def call(self, inputs):
        return self.dense(inputs)"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Ranking Models and Feature Engineering"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once candidates are retrieved, deep ranking models such as DeepFM or DLRM combine sparse categorical features (e.g., user ID, category ID) and dense numerical features (e.g., historical click-through rates, price) to predict click or conversion probabilities."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Offline Evaluation and Online Serving"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Offline evaluation utilizes historical logs to compute ranking metrics like Normalized Discounted Cumulative Gain (NDCG) and Hit Rate. For online serving, systems use low-latency databases (e.g., Redis) to store pre-computed embeddings and feature stores for real-time feature retrieval."},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"By structuring your recommendation pipeline into modular retrieval, ranking, and re-ranking phases, you ensure scalability, maintainability, and high performance in production environments."}]
